From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Dhaval Gujar <dhaval.gujar@espressif.com>
Date: Tue, 3 Nov 2026 01:55:15 +0530
Subject: [PATCH] ggl-svc: harden against the findings of a security review

An independent review of ggl-svc as a security boundary (a malicious
component, a compromised ggcore daemon, any local user) found:

- A unit file line longer than the 768-byte read buffer was parsed in
  two pieces, the second as a setting of its own. A recipe's
  ComponentDescription could thereby add X-GGL-ReadySocket=<path> to its
  unit, and root unlinked that path at every start. Lines now fit the
  whole unit file, a line that does not is rejected, values that do not
  fit their field fail the unit instead of being cut short, and only the
  core units' readiness comes from a socket, which is removed only if it
  is one.
- Unit output was forwarded with syslog(), which blocks once syslogd
  falls behind; a component writing fast enough stalled ggl-svcd and the
  IPC authentications waiting on it (whois took 6.5 s with a 1 ms/message
  syslogd). Logging now uses a non-blocking socket and waits for syslogd
  at most 100 ms per main loop pass; beyond that, a unit's output stays
  in its pipe until syslogd catches up, so a unit writing faster than
  syslogd can log is slowed down, as under journald, and nothing is
  lost. Each unit may log 1000 lines per 30 s (journald also rate-limits
  per unit); requests are served before unit output is drained.
- whois read /proc/<pid>/cgroup in 224-byte pieces and could take the
  rest of a long line for a line of its own (reachable only with cgroup
  delegation, which the board does not have).
- WorkingDirectory was entered as root; it is now entered as the unit's
  user, as systemd does.
- Units inherited signals ggl-svcd was started with as ignored (SIGQUIT,
  from a shell's background job); every signal is now reset.
- ggl-svcd could be chosen by the OOM killer; it is now exempt, as
  systemd's PID 1, and its units are not.
- A linked unit with a core unit's name is no longer loaded.

A conformance scenario added for these (the signals a unit starts with)
also showed that systemd starts services with SIGPIPE ignored
(IgnoreSIGPIPE=, true by default); ggl-svcd now does too.

Signed-off-by: Dhaval Gujar <dhaval.gujar@espressif.com>
Upstream: not yet submitted (developed on the esp32s31/v2.7.0 branch of a fork)
